# Fernly Cypress testing

Oracle: `docs/PRODUCT.md` and the OpenAPI at `/openapi.json`. When the app disagrees, keep the test expectation and record the bug in `FINDINGS.md` — never change product rules to match broken UI.

## 1. Queries

Prefer `findByRole`, then `findByLabelText`, then `findByText`. Never CSS module classes or bare tag names.

✅ **Good** — `web/cypress/component/QuantityStepper.cy.tsx`: `cy.findByRole('button', { name: 'Increase quantity' }).click()`.

❌ **Bad** — `cy.get('.button').click()` or `cy.get('button').first().click()` (mirrors `QuantityStepper.module.css` / DOM tags instead of accessible names).

## 2. States

Assert disabled, checked, or values with `.then(($el) => expect($el, 'what it is').to.be.disabled)`. A failing `.should()` chained to `findByRole` reports **Unable to find**, which looks like a broken locator when the state is wrong.

✅ **Good** — `cy.findByRole('button', { name: 'Increase quantity' }).then(($btn) => expect($btn, 'Increase quantity at stock limit').to.be.disabled);`

❌ **Bad** — `cy.findByRole('button', { name: 'Decrease quantity' }).should('be.disabled')` (masks a state failure as a missing element).

## 3. Waiting

Never `cy.wait(<milliseconds>)`. Wait for an intercepted `/api/*` request by alias (`cy.wait('@plants')`) or for what the user sees (Testing Library queries retry automatically).

✅ **Good** — `cy.findByRole('button', { name: 'Increase quantity' }).click()` then `cy.get('@onChange').should('have.been.calledWith', 3)` in `QuantityStepper.cy.tsx` (no timer; spy proves the action).

❌ **Bad** — `cy.wait(500); cy.findByRole('button', { name: 'Increase quantity' }).click()` (fixed sleep; hooks block `cy.wait(` + number in guarded files).

## 4. Data

Component props belong in the test. API answers live under `web/cypress/fixtures/` — one file per scenario (positive, negative, edge).

✅ **Good** — `cy.mount(<QuantityStepper value={3} max={3} onChange={cy.spy()} />)` with props in `QuantityStepper.cy.tsx`.

❌ **Bad** — Pasting a full catalog JSON inline in a UI spec instead of `fixture: 'plants-browse-positive.json'` (hard to reuse and review).

## 5. Mocks

`cy.intercept` only `/api/*` traffic, always with an alias. Never stub the page HTML or non-API routes.

✅ **Good** — `cy.intercept('GET', '/api/plants*', { fixture: 'plants-browse-positive.json' }).as('plants');` then `cy.wait('@plants')` before asserting browse UI (matches `apiFetch` → `/api${path}` in `web/src/api/client.ts`).

❌ **Bad** — `cy.intercept('GET', '/', { body: '<html>…</html>' })` or intercepting `http://localhost:5173/**` to fake the whole app shell.

## 6. Tags

Exactly one level tag per test: `{ tags: '@component' }` or `{ tags: '@ui' }`. A confirmed bug adds `@known-bug` (excluded from CI via `grepTags=-@known-bug` in root `package.json` until fixed).

✅ **Good** — Each `it(...)` in `QuantityStepper.cy.tsx` uses `{ tags: '@component' }` only.

❌ **Bad** — `{ tags: ['@component', '@ui'] }`, tags on `describe()`, or dropping `@known-bug` from a still-broken stock-limit test so `npm run cy:component` goes green without a fix.

## 7. App code

Never edit `web/src` to heal a test. If the test matches `docs/PRODUCT.md` and fails, add or update `FINDINGS.md`.

✅ **Good** — `FINDINGS.md` **BUG-001**: `+` stays enabled at the stock limit; evidence from `npm run cy:component` while `QuantityStepper.tsx` still uses `value > max`.

❌ **Bad** — Changing `disabled={disabled || value > max}` to `value >= max` in `web/src/components/QuantityStepper.tsx` only to satisfy `+ is disabled at the stock limit`.

## 8. Evidence

Label why an assertion is allowed: **executed** (you ran the test), **inspected** (you read PRODUCT or the contract only), or **not yet tested** (suspicion — no assertion).

✅ **Good** — `FINDINGS.md` **Evidence**: `npm run cy:component` — `+ is disabled at the stock limit` fails (executed).

❌ **Bad** — `FINDINGS.md` **Suspicions — not yet tested** entries (e.g. `$12.5` vs two decimals) turned into passing UI assertions without a run or PRODUCT line.
